# Set up the project

This walkthrough uses Deno and TypeScript to expose one RPC, call it through
Trellis, and shut down cleanly. It returns an order receipt without persisting
orders. No database, ORM, or configuration library is involved. These are
example choices, not requirements for a Trellis service.

For Rust connection and provider APIs, see the
[Rust library guide](/guides/libraries/rust). The native contract and generation
workflow are shared by both languages.

## Prerequisites

- Install [the Trellis CLI](/guides/install-trellis-cli) matching your
  libraries.
- Install Deno 2 for this example.
- For manual deployment, have a [running Trellis](/guides/starting-trellis) and
  an administrator login. The [automated test](/guides/testing-trellis-services)
  instead starts an isolated runtime using `trellis-server` and `trellis`.

These pages track the repository source. If using an older released CLI, use the
documentation from that release rather than combining authoring generations.

## Create a standalone project

```sh
mkdir orders-service
cd orders-service
deno init
deno add jsr:@qlever-llc/trellis jsr:@qlever-llc/trellis-test jsr:@std/assert
```

Select matching released versions when installing packages. Keep the import
mappings that `deno add` writes; generated code uses those package specifiers.
The test package is only needed to run the live test.

Create `trellis.toml`:

```toml
format = 1

[apis]
```

The tutorial defines its provider and caller API in the same project, so no
external API dependency is needed. `trellis.toml` is a Trellis compiler
manifest, not the service's runtime configuration file.

The resulting authored project will contain:

```text
orders-service/
  trellis.toml
  trellis.lock
  contract.trellis
  deno.json
  service.ts
  main.ts
  service_test.ts
```

## Run the checked-in example instead

The same source is checked in under
[`docs/examples/orders`](https://github.com/qlever-llc/trellis/tree/main/docs/examples/orders).
When using a Trellis source checkout, its tasks resolve that checkout's
libraries, so unreleased generated APIs can be tested without published
packages:

```sh
# From the Trellis repository root, after building the CLI:
deno task -c docs/examples/orders/deno.json generate
deno task -c docs/examples/orders/deno.json check
```

The relative paths in that example's task file are repository-only plumbing. In
a standalone service, use its installed packages and ordinary `trellis` and
`deno` commands. Continue with
[the contract](/guides/write-a-service/first-contract).
